{% doc %}
  blocks/_feature-icon.liquid — private to `blocks/_feature-card.liquid`.

  The disc in the middle of a feature card: a glyph, a filled shape behind it, a
  dashed ring around that, and a hairline running up and down to the text.

  ## The ring is an SVG, not a `border`

  `border-style: dashed` gives no control over the dash. Every browser picks its
  own length and its own gap from the width, and the design's ring is a 2px dash
  with an 8px gap — a specific rhythm that reads as stitching rather than as a
  dashed box. A `<circle>` with `stroke-dasharray` is the only way to state it,
  so **Dash** and **Dash gap** are settings rather than something a merchant has
  to accept from their browser.

  The SVG is sized in px and given a matching `viewBox`, so it draws 1:1 and the
  dash means the same thing at every ring size. `currentColor` on the stroke and
  the colour on the element is what makes the hover swap one declaration.

  ## The connectors are the card's spine

  Two hairlines, one above the ring and one below, fading out as they travel
  away from it. They are what tie the title, the icon and the text into a single
  column rather than three things that happen to be stacked.

  They are `::before` / `::after`-shaped work done with real elements instead,
  because a fading line needs a `background-image` and pseudo-elements on this
  block are already spoken for by the shape. Both are `aria-hidden`.

  **Position** is one select with four values rather than two checkboxes: a
  merchant putting the icon at the top of the card wants the line below it only,
  and two independent toggles make four states out of a question with four
  answers, two of which have to be discovered by trying.

  ## Size comes from the theme, not from this block

  `snippets/icon-size.liquid` resolves the same five keywords every icon control
  in the theme offers, from Theme settings › Colors › Icon sizes. A store that
  resizes its icons moves this one with them, and **Custom** is there for the
  design's 32px without adding a sixth vocabulary.

  ## Hover: the whole assembly turns

  Three things move on the same timing, so they read as one rotation about the
  disc rather than as three effects that happen to fire together:

    * the ring turns by **Rotation on hover**, which walks the dash around the
      circle — the reason the dash is a stated `2 8` and not the browser's guess
      is what makes that legible
    * both lines turn 180° **about the middle of the icon**, so the one above
      swings round to the bottom and the one below swings round to the top

  The lines rotate rather than travel, and that is the difference between an
  effect you can see and one you cannot. Sliding each line into the other's slot
  ends in the same place, but the path is a straight line through the disc and
  nothing on screen reads as turning. An arc around the outside of the ring is
  legible as motion at any speed.

  The 180° also lands each line upside down, which is what keeps a faded line
  fading *away* from the icon once it arrives, exactly as it did before it moved.

  All of it is one `transform` per element and one `transition`, on the card's
  own **Transition** duration. Nothing here animates a layout property, so the
  turn runs on the compositor.

  ## The turn is the one thing a phone still gets on demand

  Every other hover rule is made permanent on a touch device — see the
  `@media (hover: none)` block in `base.css`. The picture, the tint and the
  second set of colours are content, and content with no way to reach it is
  content that does not exist on a phone.

  The rotation is the exception: it stays keyed to `:active` / `:hover` /
  `:focus-within` so a tap still turns the assembly. It is the one part of the
  effect a finger can trigger, and making it permanent would spend it for
  nothing.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign icon_source = s.icon_source | default: 'icon'

  comment
    The glyph and its size, resolved to the schema's own defaults first.

    `icon-size` falls back to Medium when handed nothing, which is the right
    answer for a control that was never offered a Custom step and the wrong one
    here: this block ships on Custom at 32px, and a card with nothing stored
    would draw a 20px glyph in an 86px disc.
  endcomment
  assign icon_name = s.icon | default: 'star'
  assign icon_size_key = s.icon_size | default: 'custom'
  assign icon_size_px_custom = s.icon_size_custom | default: 32 | plus: 0

  comment
    Every colour is resolved to the schema's own default before it is used,
    rather than left to the `currentColor` fallbacks in `base.css`.

    A card whose settings were never stored — the case a preset with no
    `settings` object creates, see the note in `blocks/_feature-card.liquid` —
    reaches those fallbacks and paints a disc in the text colour. Which reads as
    the block being broken rather than as a colour nobody chose, because a disc
    is a brand mark and the theme's neutral answer is not one.

    The CSS fallbacks stay where they are. They are the answer for a stylesheet
    loaded without this block, not for this block rendering without its
    settings.
  endcomment
  assign fill_color = s.fill_color | default: '#03D26F'
  assign fill_hover_color = s.fill_hover_color | default: '#F9F871'
  assign icon_color = s.icon_color | default: '#0F2E2F'
  assign icon_hover_color = s.icon_hover_color | default: '#0F2E2F'
  assign ring_color = s.ring_color | default: '#03D26F'
  assign ring_hover_color = s.ring_hover_color | default: '#F9F871'
  assign connector_color = s.connector_color | default: '#03D26F'

  comment
    `| default:` cannot resolve a checkbox: `false | default: true` is `true` in
    every Liquid, so an unticked box would come back on. `nil` is the only value
    that means "never stored", which is the distinction these need — the same
    guard `carousel_on_mobile` uses in the section.
  endcomment
  assign connector_fade = s.connector_fade
  if connector_fade == nil
    assign connector_fade = true
  endif

  assign want_swap = s.connector_swap
  if want_swap == nil
    assign want_swap = true
  endif

  comment
    The source decides, and the field belonging to it has to actually hold
    something — a merchant who picked Image and has not uploaded one yet gets an
    empty disc rather than a broken picture. Same rule as
    `blocks/subheading.liquid`, which is where merchants learn it.
  endcomment
  assign has_icon = false
  if icon_source == 'icon' and icon_name != blank and icon_name != 'none'
    assign has_icon = true
  elsif icon_source == 'image' and s.icon_image != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: icon_size_key, custom: icon_size_px_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the shape behind the glyph ----

    `none` means the glyph sits on its own. Fill colour, size and radius are
    hidden in that case because there is no shape for them to describe, which
    is the same reasoning `icon_style` on the Subheading block is written with:
    no combination of settings that quietly does nothing.
  endcomment
  assign shape = s.shape | default: 'circle'
  assign shape_size = s.shape_size | default: 86 | plus: 0
  assign shape_radius = s.shape_radius | default: 16 | plus: 0

  comment
    A phone gets its own size. 86px is a fifth of a 400px card on a desktop and
    a quarter of the width of a 390px screen, and at that scale the disc stops
    being a mark beside the words and starts being the card.

    `| default: shape_size` rather than a constant, so a block saved before the
    mobile slider existed keeps one size at every width — the same pair, and the
    same fallback, as the two corner radii on the card.
  endcomment
  assign shape_size_mobile = s.shape_size_mobile | default: shape_size | plus: 0

  if shape == 'circle'
    assign shape_radius = shape_size
  endif

  comment
    ---- the ring ----

    Offset is the clear space between the shape and the ring, so the ring's own
    size is derived rather than asked for twice. A merchant enlarging the disc
    keeps the gap they set instead of having to correct a second number.

    The stroke is centred on the path, so the radius is inset by half the width
    or the ring is clipped by its own viewBox on both sides.
  endcomment
  assign ring_style = s.ring_style | default: 'dashed'
  assign has_ring = false
  if ring_style != 'none' and shape != 'none'
    assign has_ring = true
  endif

  assign ring_width = s.ring_width | default: 2 | plus: 0
  assign ring_offset = s.ring_offset | default: 11 | plus: 0
  assign ring_size = shape_size | plus: ring_offset | plus: ring_offset
  assign ring_size_mobile = shape_size_mobile | plus: ring_offset | plus: ring_offset
  assign ring_half = ring_size | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_radius = ring_size | minus: ring_width | divided_by: 2.0

  assign ring_inset = ring_width | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_box = ring_size | minus: ring_width

  comment
    With no ring there is no clear space to hold open, so the shape's box is the
    shape. Leaving the offset in would reserve 22px of nothing around a disc
    whose ring the merchant has just turned off, and they would go looking for a
    padding setting to correct it.
  endcomment
  unless has_ring
    assign ring_size = shape_size
    assign ring_size_mobile = shape_size_mobile
  endunless

  comment
    With no shape either, the box around the glyph is the glyph. This matters
    beyond the sizing rule in `base.css`, which is keyed off the shape modifier
    and would not fire anyway: the connector rotation below turns about the
    middle of this box, and a centre that thinks the column's middle is 86px
    tall when it is 32 sends both lines round an arc 27px off centre.
  endcomment
  if shape == 'none'
    assign ring_size = icon_size
    assign ring_size_mobile = icon_size
  endif

  comment
    Solid is the same circle with no dash pattern, rather than a second element
    or a `border`. One code path, and switching between the two styles cannot
    change anything but the dash.
  endcomment
  assign ring_dash = ''
  if ring_style == 'dashed'
    assign dash_on = s.ring_dash | default: 2 | plus: 0
    assign dash_off = s.ring_dash_gap | default: 8 | plus: 0
    assign ring_dash = dash_on | append: ' ' | append: dash_off
  endif

  comment
    ---- the connectors ----
  endcomment
  assign connectors = s.connector_position | default: 'both'
  assign connector_top = false
  assign connector_bottom = false
  if connectors == 'top' or connectors == 'both'
    assign connector_top = true
  endif
  if connectors == 'bottom' or connectors == 'both'
    assign connector_bottom = true
  endif

  assign connector_length = s.connector_length | default: 48 | plus: 0
  if connector_length <= 0
    assign connector_top = false
    assign connector_bottom = false
  endif

  comment
    ---- the swap ----

    On hover the two lines turn 180° about the middle of the icon: the one above
    swings round to the bottom, the one below swings round to the top, and the
    ring turns with them. It is one rotation of the whole assembly rather than
    two lines sliding past a disc that happens to be spinning.

    It is a rotation and not a translation, and the difference is the whole
    point. Moving each line to the other's slot lands in the same place, but the
    path is a straight line through the icon and there is nothing on screen
    that reads as turning — which is what "only the border rotates" was
    describing. An arc around the disc is legible as motion at any speed.

    Nothing here computes the geometry. `base.css` sets each line's
    `transform-origin` to the centre of the icon with a `calc()` over the
    length and ring properties this block already writes, so lengthening a line
    or resizing the disc — including at the mobile size — moves the centre with
    it and the pair still land exactly on each other. A travel distance in
    pixels could not: it would need a second value per breakpoint and a third
    the first time somebody added a gap between the parts.

    It needs both lines. With only one there is nothing to trade places with,
    and the line would swing to an empty slot and sit there — so the class is
    withheld rather than the setting hidden, and **Swap on hover** simply has
    nothing to do until Position is Above and below.
  endcomment
  assign connector_swap = false
  if connector_top and connector_bottom and want_swap
    assign connector_swap = true
  endif

  assign ring_spin = s.ring_spin | default: 180 | plus: 0

  assign block_classes = 'feature-icon-block'
  if shape != 'none'
    assign block_classes = block_classes | append: ' feature-icon-block--shape-' | append: shape
  endif
  if connector_fade
    assign block_classes = block_classes | append: ' feature-icon-block--connector-fade'
  endif
  if connector_swap
    assign block_classes = block_classes | append: ' feature-icon-block--connector-swap'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture icon_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Two sizes each, under `-desktop` and `-mobile` names, and `base.css` picks
    one into `--feature-icon-shape-size` / `--feature-icon-ring-size` at the
    breakpoint.

    The indirection is what lets everything downstream — the shape box, the disc
    box, the ring, and the `transform-origin` the connector rotation turns
    about — read one property and follow the breakpoint for free. Writing the
    mobile value into the same name instead is not an option:
    `--x: var(--x-mobile, var(--x))` is a self-reference, which resolves to the
    guaranteed-invalid value and takes the declaration down with it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --feature-icon-glyph-size: {{ icon_size }}px;
  --feature-icon-shape-size-desktop: {{ shape_size }}px;
  --feature-icon-shape-size-mobile: {{ shape_size_mobile }}px;
  --feature-icon-shape-radius: {{ shape_radius }}px;
  --feature-icon-ring-size-desktop: {{ ring_size }}px;
  --feature-icon-ring-size-mobile: {{ ring_size_mobile }}px;
  --feature-icon-connector-length: {{ connector_length }}px;
  --feature-icon-connector-width: {{ s.connector_width | default: 1 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if has_ring and ring_spin != 0 -%}
    --feature-icon-ring-spin: {{ ring_spin }}deg;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The fill and the ring colours are gated on there being something to paint,
    not just on the setting holding a value. A merchant who switches Shape to
    None has hidden those pickers but their stored colours remain, and an
    ungated `--feature-icon-fill-hover` would put a coloured square behind a
    bare glyph the moment a pointer arrived — a setting they can no longer see
    doing something they did not ask for.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if shape != 'none' -%}
    {%- if fill_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-fill: {{ fill_color }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if fill_hover_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-fill-hover: {{ fill_hover_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if icon_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-color: {{ icon_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if icon_hover_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-color-hover: {{ icon_hover_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if has_ring -%}
    {%- if ring_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-ring-color: {{ ring_color }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if ring_hover_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-ring-color-hover: {{ ring_hover_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if connector_color != blank -%}--feature-icon-connector-color: {{ connector_color }};{%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ block_classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ icon_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if connector_top -%}
    <span class="feature-icon-block__connector feature-icon-block__connector--top" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {%- endif -%}

  <span class="feature-icon-block__shape">
    {%- if has_ring -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Drawn at the desktop size with a matching viewBox, so at that width the
        dash is exactly the length the merchant asked for.

        The stylesheet stretches it to whatever box it is in, so at the mobile
        size the dash scales with the ring — a 66px disc gets a proportionally
        finer stitch rather than the desktop dash wrapped around a smaller
        circle, which is the right answer and worth knowing is what happens.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <svg
        class="feature-icon-block__ring"
        width="{{ ring_size }}"
        height="{{ ring_size }}"
        viewBox="0 0 {{ ring_size }} {{ ring_size }}"
        fill="none"
        aria-hidden="true"
        focusable="false"
      >
        {%- if shape == 'square' -%}
          <rect
            x="{{ ring_inset }}"
            y="{{ ring_inset }}"
            width="{{ ring_box }}"
            height="{{ ring_box }}"
            rx="{{ shape_radius | plus: ring_offset }}"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
            {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
          />
        {%- else -%}
          <circle
            cx="{{ ring_half }}"
            cy="{{ ring_half }}"
            r="{{ ring_radius }}"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
            {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
          />
        {%- endif -%}
      </svg>
    {%- endif -%}

    <span class="feature-icon-block__disc">
      {%- if icon_source == 'image' and s.icon_image != blank -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          An uploaded mark is content the merchant chose, so it takes their alt
          text — but it defaults to empty rather than to a filename. A feature
          card's title already names the feature; an icon repeating it is a
          second announcement of the same thing.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <img
          class="feature-icon-block__image"
          src="{{ s.icon_image | image_url: width: 160 }}"
          alt="{{ s.icon_image_alt | default: '' | escape }}"
          width="{{ icon_size }}"
          height="{{ icon_size }}"
          loading="lazy"
        >
      {%- elsif has_icon -%}
        {% render 'icon', icon: icon_name, size: icon_size, class: 'feature-icon-block__glyph' %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  </span>

  {%- if connector_bottom -%}
    <span class="feature-icon-block__connector feature-icon-block__connector--bottom" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.feature_icon.name",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_source",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "image", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__3.label" }
      ],
      "default": "icon"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "star", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "check", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "heart", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "tag", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "truck", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "refresh", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__6.label" },
        { "value": "lock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__7.label" },
        { "value": "clock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__8.label" },
        { "value": "location", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__9.label" },
        { "value": "mail", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__10.label" },
        { "value": "note", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__11.label" },
        { "value": "info", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__12.label" },
        { "value": "chat", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__13.label" },
        { "value": "phone", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__14.label" },
        { "value": "share", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__15.label" },
        { "value": "grid", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__16.label" }
      ],
      "default": "star",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "icon_image",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "icon_image_alt",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_size",
      "label": "t:shared.icon_size.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "extra_small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "medium", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "extra_large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "custom", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__6.label" }
      ],
      "default": "custom"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "icon_size_custom",
      "label": "t:shared.icon_size.custom.label",
      "info": "t:shared.icon_size.custom.info",
      "min": 12,
      "max": 72,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_size == 'custom' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "icon_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon_color.label",
      "default": "#0F2E2F"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "icon_hover_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon_hover_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon_hover_color.info",
      "default": "#0F2E2F"
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.feature_icon.headers.shape" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "shape",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.shape.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.shape.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.options.none" },
        { "value": "circle", "label": "t:shared.carousel.nav_shape.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "square", "label": "t:shared.carousel.nav_shape.options__3.label" }
      ],
      "default": "circle"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "shape_size",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.shape_size.label",
      "min": 32,
      "max": 160,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 86,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "shape_size_mobile",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.shape_size_mobile.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.shape_size_mobile.info",
      "min": 32,
      "max": 160,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 66,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "shape_radius",
      "label": "t:shared.corners.radius.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 48,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 16,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape == 'square' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "fill_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.fill_color.label",
      "default": "#03D26F",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "fill_hover_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.fill_hover_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.fill_hover_color.info",
      "default": "#F9F871",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape != 'none' }}"
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.feature_icon.headers.ring" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.feature_icon.paragraphs.ring" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "ring_style",
      "label": "t:shared.border.style.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.options.none" },
        { "value": "solid", "label": "t:shared.options.solid" },
        { "value": "dashed", "label": "t:shared.options.dashed" }
      ],
      "default": "dashed",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.shape != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "ring_offset",
      "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.ring_offset.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.ring_offset.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 1,
